{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>Let me cross over, pray, and see the good land, etc.</b> Why did he interrupt with the word ״נָא״ between ״אֶעְבְּרָה״ and ״וְאֶרְאֶה״, and why did he make two divisions, mentioning ״הָאָרֶץ״ on its own and ״הָהָר הַטּוֹב הַזֶּה וְהַלְּבָנוֹן״ on their own? And the explanation of the matter is that Moses asked for two things: one on behalf of Israel, and one on his own behalf. And the answer came: ״וַיִּתְעַבֵּר ה׳ בִּי לְמַעַנְכֶם״ — for in the matter touching you, HaShem did not grant my request; but what touches me, He did grant me, and said: ״רַב לָךְ״ — it is enough that this thing be yours alone; would you further seek on their behalf as well? And these two things are the two kinds of perfection that HaShem bestowed upon Abraham. For at first HaShem said to Abraham: ״שָׂא נָא עֵינֶיךָ וּרְאֵה מִן הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה שָׁם צָפֹנָה וָנֶגְבָּה וְגוֹ׳, כִּי לְךָ אֶתְּנֶנָּה וּלְזַרְעֲךָ עַד עוֹלָם״ (Genesis 13:14–15) — hear from this that by vision alone he acquired it. But afterward He retracted from this and said: ״קוּם הִתְהַלֵּךְ בָּאָרֶץ לְאָרְכָּהּ וּלְרׇחְבָּהּ, כִּי לְךָ אֶתְּנֶנָּה״ (ibid. 13:17) — hear from this that by vision alone He did not wish him to acquire it, but only when he would walk its borders and take hold of it by a superior act of possession. Moreover, He did not say ״וּלְזַרְעֲךָ עַד עוֹלָם״ there, but only ״לְךָ״ alone. And what is the meaning of what He said: ״וּרְאֵה מִן הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה שָׁם״ — would you really think that he would see from a place where he is not standing? And furthermore, there the language of ״נָא״ is used: ״שָׂא נָא עֵינֶיךָ וּרְאֵה״ — specifically for the thing acquired by vision, and not for the thing acquired by complete possession. And here it says: ״אֶעְבְּרָה נָא״, and the language of ״נָא״ is mentioned specifically for the thing that requires an actual crossing on foot — this is self-evident." "<b>And look to the right and see, for from the Holy Land there is physical and spiritual benefit.</b> The physical benefit is that which depends on the entire area of the land — to eat of its fruit and be sated from its goodness, for it is a land flowing with milk and honey. And this is the thing that is not acquired except by actual possession, when one walks its borders, as is the law for every purchaser of a field (Bava Kamma 9), and concerning this it was said to Abraham: ״קוּם הִתְהַלֵּךְ בָּאָרֶץ לְאָרְכָּהּ וּלְרׇחְבָּהּ״, and in this He did not use the language of ״נָא״, for ״נָא״ is nothing but a request. And since man's material nature is by its nature drawn toward the love of physical pleasures, for every man who desires life loves days that he may see good, therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, did not need to speak in the language of ״נָא״, the language of request, for on the contrary, man requests it from Him in the language of ״נָא״ — for so Moses said concerning this acquisition that requires crossing, in the language of ״נָא״, as it says: ״אֶעְבְּרָה נָא״. And when man requests it from HaShem, surely the Holy One, blessed be He, does not need to request it from man — the one depends on the other. And in this acquisition He did not mention ״לְךָ וּלְזַרְעֲךָ עַד עוֹלָם״, for the land is not acquired for his seed forever in any case, since it is given on condition — if they keep His statutes and observe His teachings." "And the spiritual benefit does not come from the area of the land as a whole, but only from this good mountain and the Lebanon below, which is aligned opposite the heavenly Temple, and there is the dwelling of the power of the Shekhinah, and the place is called: ״ה׳ יִרְאֶה״ (ibid. 22:14), because everyone who sees that holy place by vision alone acquires perfection for his soul, and is clothed in a spirit of holiness and purity, for in the King's beauty shall his eyes behold Him — for just as one comes to see there, so too one comes to be seen. And concerning this acquisition He said to him: ״שָׂא נָא עֵינֶיךָ וּרְאֵה מִן הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה שָׁם״ etc., for at that time Abraham was in Bethel, and Bethel — that is the Temple, for from the Temple the abundance goes out to all four directions of the world, for from that well all the spirits drink, as was explained above in parashat Lekh Lekha (13:17). And since for the spiritual benefit man is by nature not drawn toward it, therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: ״שָׂא נָא״, the language of request, in the manner that they said concerning the first man: entice him to enter the Garden of Eden — for man needs enticement for the acceptance of any spiritual thing whose pleasure is not felt immediately. And correspondingly Moses said: ״וְאֶרְאֶה״, and did not say ״וְאֶרְאֶה נָא״, the language of request, for man by his nature does not seek this so much; on the contrary, the Holy One, blessed be He, needs to request it from him. And even though among the perfected ones such as Moses and Abraham it is not applicable to say thus, nevertheless they spoke in the general language of the children of men. And concerning this acquisition HaShem said to Abraham: ״וּלְזַרְעֲךָ עַד עוֹלָם״, for that holy place exists forever in the persistence of its holiness, since it is aligned opposite the heavenly Temple. And even if this one below is destroyed, nevertheless that place remains aligned opposite the heavenly Temple, pouring holiness upon that place, and forever in every single generation this holiness has not departed — for behold, in the days of the Patriarchs there was no built Temple there, and even so the holiness was present. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Yoma 72a): ״עֲצֵי שִׁטִּים עֹמְדִים״ — that they stand for all eternities. And in tractate Megillah (28a) our Rabbis of blessed memory said from the verse: ״וַהֲשִׁמּוֹתִי אֶת מִקְדְּשֵׁיכֶם״ (Leviticus 26:31), that even in their desolation, nevertheless in their holiness they stand — that is to say, in their original holiness nothing is lacking, as it is written: ״נִכְבָּדוֹת מְדֻבָּר בָּךְ עִיר הָאֱלֹהִים סֶלָה״ (Psalms 87:3), and the word ״סֶלָה״ indicates eternity." "And with this proposal everything is resolved, for Moses asked for both: for when he said ״אֶעְבְּרָה נָא״, he asked for the actual acquisition of the land to grant it to Israel in complete possession, and in this he used the language of ״נָא״, for everything given to man in the acquisitions of this world is solely lovingkindness, and he did not ask for this crossing on his own behalf but only on behalf of Israel in his lovingkindness — for if he would bequeath the land to them it would become theirs permanently. And afterward he asked also on his own behalf and said: ״וְאֶרְאֶה״, for on his own behalf he asked only for the spiritual perfection that is acquired by vision. And then he explained each one in order: what I said ״אֶעְבְּרָה נָא״, that is ״אֶת הָאָרֶץ הַטּוֹבָה״, for the purpose of acquiring the whole land in its entirety for Israel. And what I said ״וְאֶרְאֶה״, that is ״הָהָר הַטּוֹב הַזֶּה וְהַלְּבָנוֹן״, for the spiritual perfection within it is acquired by vision. And he interrupted with the word ״נָא״ so that the language of ״נָא״ would not recur except for the crossing, for the reason explained above." "<b>But HaShem was wrathful with me for your sakes.</b> Regarding the matter that I requested for your sakes — to acquire the land for you — HaShem did not grant my request, and HaShem said to me ״רַב לָךְ״ — enough in this, when I grant your request that is for you alone, namely the vision. And concerning this He said: ״עֲלֵה רֹאשׁ הַפִּסְגָּה וְשָׂא עֵינֶיךָ יָמָּה וְצָפֹנָה״ etc. And you will discern and know that with Abraham He began with the northward direction, while here He began with westward, because the essence of this vision that imparts extra holiness is through seeing the Shekhinah that is in the Western Wall, as it is written (Isaiah 59:19) ״וְיִרְאוּ מִמַּעֲרָב אֶת שֵׁם ה׳״. But Abraham, who stood in that place and spoke of the abundance that goes forth from there to all four directions, began with the north direction, which is dark and needs most to receive the abundance." "And as for why HaShem did not desire that Moses bequeath them the Land — for so it concludes (Deuteronomy Rabbah 8:1) that prayer, if it does not accomplish all of it, accomplishes half. And so Moses' prayer on behalf of Aaron was effective for half; likewise here he was granted half of his request, and the physical request was displaced in favor of the spiritual. And from the fact that He said 'for your sake,' infer from it that Israel were not worthy that Moses bequeath it to them, for the face of Moses is like the face of the sun — like this sun, whose light is constant without interruption, so all the gifts of Moses are eternal, and the acquisition of Torah through him will testify. But Joshua is like the moon, whose light is not constant; so too the Land that he bequeathed them — sometimes, when they merit, there is light for all the children of Israel in their settlements, and when they do not merit — ״רָאִיתִי הָאָרֶץ וְהִנֵּה תֹהוּ״ (Jeremiah 4:23), ״צַר וָאוֹר חָשַׁךְ בַּעֲרִיפֶיהָ״ (Isaiah 5:30)." "And some say that Moses requested to see the Land, so that he would not be included in ״וְכָל מְנַאֲצַי לֹא יִרְאוּהָ״ (Numbers 14:23). And some say: ״רַב לָךְ״ — the word ״לָךְ״ has the numerical value of fifty, for Moses himself said ״יְמֵי שְׁנוֹתֵינוּ בָהֶם שִׁבְעִים שָׁנָה״ (Psalms 90:10), and he had already lived one hundred and twenty years, that is, fifty more than what was fitting by the way of nature. And now he sought to live longer; therefore He said to him: it is enough for you — those fifty years that were added to you." "And some say that Moses said: the decree was only that he would not bring the congregation etc., and since Joshua leads them, if so he may enter the Land as one of the people. And He answered him ״רַב לָךְ״ in astonishment — is it fitting for you that your disciple sit and deliver the sermon while you sit and watch?"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {5 "The abbreviation בדב״ר is resolved as במדבר רבה, with the citation (ח א) pointing to parashah 8, chapter 1."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:05.239548239Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}